---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About us"
  description="The philosophy behind Proof of Worth: low time preference from Bitcoin economics meets Buy-It-For-Life consumption."
>
  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="container article">
      <span class="eyebrow">Philosophy</span>
      <h1>Time preference as a compass for consumption</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Proof of Worth connects the concept of low time preference from Bitcoin economics
        with the practical consumption philosophy of "Buy It For Life" (BIFL).
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container article">
      <h2>Why fiat money encourages high time preference</h2>
      <p>
        Money that continuously loses purchasing power changes behavior: saving is
        punished, immediate consumption becomes relatively more attractive. This economic
        environment favors products built for quick sale rather than durability – planned
        obsolescence included.
      </p>
      <p>
        Anyone who instead thinks in a hard, deflationary unit like Bitcoin weighs
        spending differently: every expense has an opportunity cost. The question becomes
        less "Can I afford this?" and more "How many sats does this cost me – and what
        will it still be worth in 10 years?"
      </p>

      <h2>Buy It For Life as a practical answer</h2>
      <p>
        BIFL isn't a brand or a seal, but an attitude: buying products that are
        repairable, robust, and available long-term – instead of disposable goods that
        end up in the trash shortly after purchase. Proof of Worth makes this attitude
        concrete by rating every product against traceable criteria
        (see the <a href="/en/score-methodik">score methodology</a>).
      </p>

      <h2>No greenwashing, only physical criteria</h2>
      <p>
        Instead of trusting marketing seals, Proof of Worth relies on verifiable facts:
        repairability, material quality, warranty length, and resale value. Every rating
        is backed by sources and traceable through the Git history of the
        <a href="/en/mitmachen">GitHub repository</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>An open project</h2>
      <p>
        This site is deliberately built as a community-maintained directory – not a
        closed editorial team. Colleagues and users contribute new products, corrections,
        and long-term experiences via GitHub. More on that under
        <a href="/en/mitmachen">Contribute</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .article { max-width: 720px; }
  .article h2 { margin-top: 2rem; }
</style>
